Date: Thu, 14 Oct 93 08:31:31 BST
From: jrk@sys.uea.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Seasonal Affective Disorder: my quick fix

Dan Davis writes:
>I've installed track lighting above my bed with 3 75 watt directional
>bulbs aimed where my head lies, and set the whole thing on a timer so
>that the sun rises promptly at 9:20 every morning (nothing like grad
>school). It does help me wake up. Would probably work even better with
>a bright comforter to reflect the light better.

I don't get SAD, but I do find that I tend to sleep longer when the nights
are longer.  I've adopted much the same solution: a single 75W light over
my bed on a time switch that turns it on at about 5am.  (My preferred
rising time is around 6.  It is a truly wonderful feeling to go for an
early morning stroll over dark, silent, snow-covered fields in midwinter,
knowing that everyone else is still lying unconscious in their beds.)

However, I feel it would work better if it didn't turn the light on
suddenly, but faded it up gradually over, say, 20 minutes.  This has also
been found in experiments on the circadian rhythms of animals: entraining
them to an artificial cycle of light and darkness is much more effective if
the turn-on and turn-off are gradual.  I don't know if this has also been
found in people, but it would be interesting to experiment.

Can anyone suggest a circuit for doing this?  I'm familiar with the basic
dimmer circuit controlled by a mechanical potentiometer, and can easily
make a circuit which will ramp a DC control voltage from 0 to peak in 20
minutes, but cannot think of a simple way of using that signal to control a
dimmer.  All I can think of is to replace the potentiometer with an
analogue optocoupler.  Any electronic engineers here with suggestions for a
voltage-controlled dimmer circuit?

