Just saw "Demolition Man" and was inspired.)

There are many flavors of "Webster's Dictionaries," the name "Webster"
having passed into the public domain many, many years ago. I have seen
pieces of utter garbage on sale in B. Dalton and Waldenbooks with the name
"Webster's" across it. From Kennita's comments, it sounds like she got one
of these turkeys.

The _real_ Webster's is the very large Merriam-Webster Third, which retails
for about $80-$100. I have one, but almost never use it anymore.

(Merriam-Webster also publishes several smaller dictionaries.)

Other dictionaries I have and some comments:

* Oxford English Dictionary (OED), New Edition. I have the OED II in
photoreduced form, so a magnifier is essential. Far and away the most
pleasurable dictionary to browse. (The CD-ROM version would be wonderful,
but goes for about $800 minimum, whereas the version I got was $260. A
Newton-type gizmo  with a large dictionary, say, 30 MB of definitions and
synthesized pronunciations, will someday arrive.)

A total of 21,000 pages, by the way.

A 2-volume abridged version of the OED is now out, and looks quite nice. I
didn't catch the price, but the bookstores all seem to have it--if it's
less than $150 and you really like words, give it a look. (The OED folks
fully computerized for the OED II and are now proliferating
versions...looks like they're making a run for dominance of the American
market.)

* American Heritage Dictionary, Third Edition. (Not the College Edition!).
About $40. Outstanding layout and typography. Etymologies and Indo-European
roots. This is the dictionary I would recommend to anyone who only has one
of those execrable (look it up) little pocket dictionaries.

* Random House Unabridged, Second Edition. Once my favorite, I use it less
often now. About $80.


Well, that's it. I clearly have too many dictionaries...and I have some
smaller versions I haven't even mentioned. One would be enough. I'm not
sure why I keep buying new ones.


