Subject: RECOVERY: What's Glove Got to Do With It? 

I always hated sports in school but about a year ago took up Karate. I
thoroughly recommend that people take up a martial art by the way: I
would like the ability to carry a gun at all times legaly but I would
still train against not having it.

Regarding sports training kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson) writes:

> Certainly your mileage may vary.  My guesses are that important
> factors are a preponderance of novices, a patient coach, and a lot of
> positive attitudes.

I don't think the preponderance of novices is that important. Our dojo
is only about 30% beginners (less than 1 year) at any given time. The
important thing for me is that people be judged according to their
grade. For a while I was concerned that each time I would correct an
error a new one would replace it. Then I looked around at the people
of a lower grade they were making these "new" errors: you don't get
criticised for failing to do difficult things until you are correctly
doing simple things. Hence you are only dealing with one problem at a
time and don't get swamped.

Yes, there a lot of positive attitude but there danger of dismissing
it as empty praise even if you were inclined to do so.

"this is hard, don't worry about it if you can't do it at first"
"everyone starts at at the bottom"
"we [black belts] have faults too [example]"

