Subject: Seasonal Affective Disorder...

Phil G. Fraering writes:

>Just thought I'd mention: not everyone has Seasonal Affective
>Disorder where *winter* is the "depressive" season.

>Charlie, are you in England right now or still in Santa Cruz?
>If you're in Santa Cruz, is the SAD as strong as in Britian?
>Did you suddenly go back after spending a bunch of time at
>a lower latitude, and have the daylight available decreace
>a whole lot at once?

Back in England. To recap: I spent most of September in California.
Flew back to England on the 2nd of October. After prolonged jet-lag
(aggravated by going in to work the next day) I noticed that my
normal seasonal depression, which tends to set in around the end
of October, had set in a few weeks earlier and considerably harder
than usual. I suspect the higher longitude/shorter days and colder
(positively dank) weather had something to do with it. As for the
light level ... if I said that this was already the wettest October
on record in the UK, that would give you some idea what the cloud
cover's like!

My personal impression is that this syndrome is triggered by reducing
the length of daylight hours and reducing the intensity of daylight.
It feels just like staying up too late at night. Most annoying. Do
any of you living in climes nearer to the equator get this problem?

(I think this weekend I'm going to start pricing 1000 watt halogen
floodlights for my living room ...)


