From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Public School Sports

Kennita's Dynamically Optimistic post about softball being better than
the team sports in school leads me to suggest that most of the problems
of school sports are caused by their statist nature.

To begin with, school attendance is legally compulsory, unfortunately.
With regard to required P.E. classes, this means that people who don't
want to be there are forced to participate.  In my experience this always
made things worse for those that wanted to play the game.

Also, Physical Education is structured to provide a physically fit pool
from which conscripts can be drawn in wartime.  This means that there tends
to be an emphasis upon warlike, team sports, rather than sports in which
people play alone.  Also, it means that there tends to be an emphasis upon
win-lose sports, rather than win-win or sports that are for the development
of fitness and character for the individual's sake, not the sake of others,
the team, the school, etc.

This paradigm of socializing students to become good potential military
conscripts also tends to extend to "extra-curricular" "intermural" sports.
Football is probably the best contemporary example.  Team and school "spirit"
is encouraged to socialize students into an ideology of doing things for
a cause which doesn't benefit them tangibly or materially, much as does
nationalism/patriotism.  The benefits involved tend not to be intangibles
or immaterial for the individual, but tangible, material benefits for
others.

If I designed a physical education curriculum, I would probably place
heavy emphasis upon the most defensive martial arts as well as upon
"solo" sports that are more oriented towards competing against yourself
in order to improve your performance, your health, etc.  In other words,
I'd make self-improvement the primary goal, including the sheer fun
of playing the game!

