Date:     Thu, 14 Oct 93 9:53:35 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Archery

One thing that ought to be noted about the longbows that the English used
at Agincourt is that their draw was heavy enough that users didn't pause
while holding the string back for very long unless they were very strong.
120 pounds is the weight I recall for their draw.  They were fired by
drawing them and firing immediately, which is why they were so hard to
learn to fire accurately.

As for the accuracy and range of modern crossbows, I would suspect that
it all depends upon the draw weight.  The Trident pistol bow Barnett makes
has about a 50 pound draw, as I recall.  I wouldn't trust this to be
accurate at much more than 25 yards.  However, 200-250 pound draws for
crossbows aren't uncommon.  A friend of mine had one with a little over
a hundred pound draw, and he was fairly confident about being able to
hit archery bullseyes at 100 yards.

Then there's the "crossbow" that fires a bolt with CO2 under high pressure,
claiming the equivalent of a 400 pound draw(!).  I'd love to field-test
one of those to see just how accurate it was.  Might even need a scope.

However, when compared to sniper rifles than can fire 2 miles with
high accuracy, any archery falls short (so to speak).

