-------------orginal letter------------------------------
What follows is from memory so you should take it with a pinch of salt until 
it is confirmed from the literature. 

A good introduction to SAD was published in Scientific American a couple of 
years ago written by the original researchers who coined the term (sorry I 
can't remember the actual reference).

> Nobody's quite sure where SAD comes from, but there are a couple of
> postulated causes. One is a residual hibernation reflex from somewhere
> _way_ down the family tree. A more credible answer is a prehistoric 
> adaptation to survival in the north (near the Arctic circle) during
> winter, when game is scarce. Whatever causes it, it plays merry hell
> with me and I want to get rid of it.

Both these hypotheses are almost certainly wrong.

A great many processes in the body appear to follow rhythms (e.g. body 
temperature, release of Growth Hormone, etc.). These process seem to be 
associated with one of two clocks: one associated with core-body temperature 
(amongst other things) and the other with the sleep-wake cycle. 

Normally these cycles are approximately 24 hours long and hence are called 
circadian rhythms ('circa' means about; 'dies' means day). However it has 
been shown that in the absence of external stimuli - most notably light - 
these clocks follow patterns longer than 24 hours. Since the two cycles have 
different periods when not entrained they get out of sync with each other. 
This may cause many of the symptoms associated with SAD such as depression. 

It is interesting to note that the person who broke the world isolation 
record - down a cave in constant illumination - suffered depression for 
months after she returned to the surface and eventually suicided. 

It is argued that in winter many people close to the poles suffer from this 
de-synchronisation to their biological clocks caused by light levels 
dropping and an increase in the amount of time spent inside (which usually 
does not have adequate illumination to entrain your biological clocks). 
Hence the logical solution for SAD sufferers is to shine a bright light in 
their face! I am not sure how long you need to shine the light or how bright 
it needs to be (1000-2000 lux ???) or exactly where in your circadian cycle 
it is best to do so - you need to read the literature. But I would be 
surprised if you need to match the spectrum of the sun closely - I think its 
the overall illumination that is important.

BTW: there is now alot of evidence that light can be used quickly change 
sleep patterns for the jet-lagged or shift workers. From memory one study 
showed that you could reset the body's clocks in a matter of days as opposed 
to weeks for people with severe jet-lag. 

Hope this helps,
